## Fresh read-only AWS result

The default AWS profile matched the expected account. Exactly five AWS application
commands completed: one account check, two object HEADs and two conditional GETs.
Automatic retries were disabled; no request was retried. The bounded operation
took approximately 6.171 seconds under a 60-second application deadline.

Only the two exact inventory-bound checkpoint metadata objects were downloaded:

| Object | Bytes | SHA-256 |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| `distributed-training-state.json` | 7,583 | `819046f39176dc9fce677db69623265e83a5c6dbdd38bed78fec8adaff1fb3e6` |
| `manifest.sha256` | 1,564 | `73c852095441a4246cea7bb97f7831c364137869e00e29c815b7e22a5972f8de` |

The exact bucket/key selection came from the independently hash-bound Segment-3
backup plan. Each HEAD required the historical size; each GET was conditioned on
the observed ETag and required the original content SHA-256. Total body bytes:
**9,147**. ETag was a concurrency condition, not used as a content hash. The
operation bounds were five commands, 16 KiB response-metadata allowance per
command, 15 seconds per command, and no weight/trainer/data object access.

This confirms those two objects, not current availability or contents of all
52 backup objects. Network traffic was not packet-metered. No credentials or raw
stderr were retained; no remote object, bucket, instance or registry was changed.

Exact downloaded bytes are retained privately in
`artifacts/lumi-sft-final-base-metadata-v0.1/`, directory 0700, files 0400.
Saved sizes/hashes were rechecked. Later checks reused these bytes without
another S3 GET. The checkpoint state may contain historical operational metadata;
it is not a Git/publication target and is not reproduced in the aggregate report.

