## Corrected tokenizer metadata interpretation

The v0.69 statement that an expected tokenizer checksum file was missing was
incorrect. The preserved field named `tokenizer.manifest_sha256` has a different
documented meaning: it hashes a normalized JSON inventory, not a line-format
`manifest.sha256` file.

The original implementation `src/lumi/posttraining_readiness.py:72` normalizes
the three entries to `bytes`, `relative_path`, and `sha256`, sorts by path, and
hashes compact sorted-key UTF-8 JSON without a trailing newline. The readiness
script also matches that inventory against the backup-plan objects. Its exact
425-byte canonical representation yields
`282d7caa5a9a952c8af66fb28679ee34fa3c844c28e8922f4b4e297ca0fdaab9`.

The synthetic import-v2 validator instead checks that digest against a supplied
checksum-line file, then parses checksum lines. That is a genuine real-lineage
contract mismatch. Existing tiny synthetic tests did not catch it because their
fixtures used checksum-file semantics. Neither original source nor prior tests
were changed or falsely relabeled as real-base acceptance.

Added the pure, independently pinned metadata validator
`src/lumi/posttraining_final_base_metadata_v1.py` and synthetic tests. It accepts
only four bounded byte strings: base binding, DCP manifest, checkpoint state and
model configuration. It uses the original tokenizer inventory algorithm, requires
the caller's independent base-file hash before parsing, preserves or strengthens
the original DCP/state/config/run/rank checks, and returns only fixed metadata
fields. It cannot open files, access a network, import ML, restore a checkpoint
or dispatch a worker. Input limits: base/manifest/config 16 KiB each, state 32 KiB,
combined 64 KiB, at most 128 manifest entries, report at most 4 KiB.

The four exact real metadata inputs total **12,048 bytes** and pass this new
validator. An independent evaluation of the original pure readiness definitions
agrees on the tokenizer inventory hash. All input/source hashes were verified
before and after. The original immutable importer is not wired to this successor;
a real-import executor must explicitly integrate it later. No tokenizer checksum
file was fabricated, and no synthetic size limit was widened.

Tokenizer payload files were not read. Thus inventory lineage is verified while
`tokenizer_payload_verified=false`, `checkpoint_payload_verified=false`,
`real_4b_import=false` and all execution flags stay false. The pure validator's
`remote_availability_verified=false` means it performs no remote observation;
the separate acquisition receipt records the successful two-object AWS check.

## Tests and source bindings

Forty-two new synthetic/source tests cover the original algorithm, all six input
inventory permutations, auxiliary-field normalization, checksum-file and newline
confusion, wrong pins, malformed/duplicate JSON, bounded reads, manifest/path/
rank/state/config mismatches, strict numeric types and closed non-execution flags.
Tests preserve 12 core original predicate ASTs and map every prior lineage check.
No test opens private metadata or model/tokenizer/data payloads.

Together with the existing launch-v2, import-v2 and WorkerRun-v1 regressions,
**167 tests pass without failures/skips** on Python 3.11.15 (0.193s), 3.13.7
(0.209s) and 3.14.6 (0.198s). Exact Ruff E,F,I,B,UP, line length 100, target py311
passes. Independent read-only source review found no concrete blocker. No native
Linux acceptance, ML test, CI workflow or GPU run was started.

